---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const H = {
  short: "How a plugin's actions and upgrades are tested",
  how: "Action tests, and upgrade tests",
  example: "A published antisense oligonucleotide, as a test",
  rules: "Where they run",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A plugin's <code>ActionTest</code> documents run one action on given values through <code
        >iki-worker run --local</code
      >, with no server, and compare its output with what the test expects. <code
        >iki plugin test --upgrade-from REF</code
      > builds the plugin as it was at an earlier commit and as it is now, makes sample data with
      the earlier build, upgrades it, compares every revision's values, and rolls back. In CI, one
      test runs every first-party plugin's action tests, and the template's (<Adr n={[93, 94, 158]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      Action tests live in a plugin's <code>tests/</code> directory. Each names an action, its
      params and inputs, and the output it expects: the entities it creates or updates, with their
      values, numbers within a relative tolerance unless the test sets one. <code>iki plugin test</code>{" "}
      checks each against the plugin, makes the run input the server would give the action's
      command, runs it through the worker, and says where the output differs (<Path
        p="spec/src/iki_spec/action_tests.py"
      />, <Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/testing.py" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      An upgrade test builds the plugin at the earlier commit and now, and runs three steps in a
      scratch PostgreSQL database: <code>before</code> makes the samples from the earlier commit's
      action tests, <code>after</code> upgrades them with the current build, and <code>rollback</code>{" "}
      goes back with the earlier one. Each sample is made in two revisions, and each revision's values
      must come through as its migrations say, renamed or converted, and otherwise as they were
      (<Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/upgrade_test.py" />, <Path
        p="server/tests/test_plugin_migrations.py"
        sym="def test_an_upgrade_keeps_each_revisions_values_as_its_migrations_say("
      />). How migrations keep history is on <PageLink to="Migrations and versions" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      The oligo plugin's tests register nusinersen from its HELM notation and expect the formula,
      weights and modifications its published sources give, each cited where it's from. What it expects:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/oligo/tests/oligos.yaml" start="^expect:" note="the first test, nusinersen" />
    <p>
      <code>iki plugin test plugins/oligo</code> runs it locally; in CI it runs in the worker shard.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Every first-party plugin's action tests, in one test" open>
        <p>
          One parametrized test finds every first-party plugin with an action test, and the template,
          and runs each with the plugins it depends on. The template's runs through the test skill it
          ships to plugin authors, as a lab would (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_worker_local.py"
            sym="def test_first_party_plugins_and_the_template_pass_their_action_tests("
          />, <Path p="plugin-template/.agents/skills/iki-test-plugin" />). It needs iki-worker built,
          so it's a worker test, in CI's worker shard (<PageLink to="Gates and CI" hash="rules" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A plugin's own repository">
        <p>
          The plugin template's CI downloads the released iki-worker, refuses it unless Iki's
          release workflow attested it, and runs <code>iki plugin test</code> with it, with <code
            >--upgrade-from</code
          > the pull request's base when there is one (<Path p="plugin-template/.github/workflows/ci.yml" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Components and migrations">
        <p>
          A plugin's web components are tested in their frames, in three browsers (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_oligo_components.py"
          />, <PageLink to="Browser tests" />). Its migrations are made and run on PostgreSQL
          by <Path p="server/tests/test_plugin_migrations.py" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/testing.py" />: <code>iki plugin test</code></li>
        <li><Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/upgrade_test.py" />: <code>--upgrade-from</code></li>
        <li><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/action_tests.py" />: what an action test says, and how it's compared</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0093" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0094" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0158" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
